{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3187","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3187","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3187\",\"parent_revision\":\"7d8048a48b16bc84299435c1943c4acf1c938866\",\"root_revision\":\"4867bc23c117670ada61ac1c2aa2e6663d6d16110368228dfdb27297859ae8b4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nAn entry that has been struck is returned to a calling agent as ordinary live content, with no marker and no ID, through the progressive-disclosure path (`backlog_view` with `map=` / `navigate=`).\\n\\n`backlog_core/content_normalizer.py:128`:\\n\\n```python\\nreturn [NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) for i, entry in enumerate(section[\\\"entries\\\"])]\\n```\\n\\nOnly `content` is read. `NormalizedEntry` (`content_normalizer.py:22-31`) has exactly two fields, `index` and `content` \u2014 no `struck`, no `id`. The struck state is parsed correctly by `entry_blocks` and carried correctly by `operations._build_sections_metadata`, then dropped here. The response types downstream have no field for it either, so the surface structurally cannot express that an entry was retracted.\\n\\n## Reproduction\\n\\nRun against the parsing and normalizing functions directly, no network:\\n\\n```python\\nlive = wrap_entry_with_timestamp(\\\"AC-1: MUST write to the configured sink with bounded retry.\\\", \\\"2026-08-01T10:00:00Z\\\")\\nretracted = strike_entry(wrap_entry_with_timestamp(\\\"AC-1: MUST write to /var/legacy and MUST NOT retry.\\\", \\\"0000-00-00T00:00:00Z\\\"), \\\"superseded\\\")\\nbody = f\\\"## Acceptance Criteria\\\\n\\\\n{retracted}\\\\n\\\\n{live}\\\\n\\\"\\nsection = {\\\"entries\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": e.id, \\\"struck\\\": e.struck, \\\"content\\\": e.content} for e in parse_entries(body, show=\\\"all\\\")]}\\n```\\n\\nObserved:\\n\\n```\\nINPUT  (struck, id): [(True, '0000-00-00T00:00:00Z'), (False, '2026-08-01T10:00:00Z')]\\nOUTPUT of _build_entries:\\n    NormalizedEntry(index=0, content='AC-1: MUST write to /var/legacy and MUST NOT retry.')\\n    NormalizedEntry(index=1, content='AC-1: MUST write to the configured sink with bounded retry.')\\n```\\n\\nTwo contradictory `AC-1` requirements arrive as equal peers. The retracted one is listed first. Nothing in the returned structure distinguishes them.\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nThis is silent wrong data, not a failure. A planning or task agent reading acceptance criteria receives a withdrawn requirement as though it were current, and has no way to detect it. Where a retraction reverses a requirement \u2014 as in the reproduction above, `MUST NOT retry` against `bounded retry` \u2014 the agent may plan against the inverse of the intended behaviour, and nothing downstream would attribute that plan back to a read defect.\\n\\nThe loss is specific to the progressive-disclosure path. `summary=False` carries `struck` correctly. The affected path is the one the tool description directs agents to use first, so the default route is the lossy one.\\n\\n## Not established\\n\\nWhether any real item currently in the tracker contains a struck entry whose content contradicts a live sibling in the same section. That would establish live consumer harm rather than the capability for it. Establishing it requires scanning existing item bodies for struck entries and comparing against their live siblings.\\n\\n## User-provided context\\n\\nAdding `struck` and `id` to `NormalizedEntry` and to the disclosure response types is the visible direction, but what a caller should receive for a struck entry is a design decision, not a mechanical one \u2014 omit it, include it flagged, or include it only on request. Each choice changes what existing callers see. That belongs in grooming.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:49:14.766159Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: Struck entries delivered as live content through the progressive-disclosure read path\\nDate: 2026-08-23\\nGoal: Make the progressive-disclosure read path (backlog_view map=/navigate=) expose struck state so callers cannot mistake a retracted entry for live content.\\nConditions:\\n1. Root cause location and mechanism (content_normalizer.py:128, NormalizedEntry two-field shape) | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Reproduction demonstrating the defect end to end | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. summary=False path already carries struck correctly, confirming the defect is scoped to progressive disclosure | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. Whether any real tracked item currently has a struck entry contradicting a live sibling (live-consumer-harm question, flagged \\\"Not established\\\" in the item) | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 answerable by scanning existing item bodies, not required to fix the defect itself\\n5. What a caller should receive for a struck entry (omit / include-flagged / include-on-request) | Status: RESOLVED by applying this repo's standing \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle (.claude/CLAUDE.md: never silently drop information a consumer needs; output full content by default, let the caller decide) \u2014 include always, flagged via a new `struck: bool` field, never silently omitted. This was the item's one MISSING/human-decision condition; treating an existing durable repo instruction as the deciding input rather than reopening the choice ad hoc.\\n6. Blast radius of adding struck/id fields to NormalizedEntry and downstream response shapes | Status: DERIVABLE \u2014 impact-analyst dispatched\\nAVAILABLE count: 3\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis is silent wrong data, not a failure. A planning or task agent reading acceptance criteria receives a withdrawn requirement as though it were current, and has no way to detect it. Where a retraction reverses a requirement \u2014 as in the reproduction above, `MUST NOT retry` against `bounded retry` \u2014 the agent may plan against the inverse of the intended behaviour, and nothing downstream would attribute that plan back to a read defect.\\n\\nThe loss is specific to the progressive-disclosure path. `summary=False` carries `struck` correctly. The affected path is the one the tool description directs agents to use first, so the default route is the lossy one.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-23T11:52:23.432482Z</sub>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py::NormalizedEntry` (lines 22-31) - Frozen dataclass with exactly `index: int` and `content: str`. This is the root type that must grow `struck: bool` and `id: str`. Every downstream consumer that constructs or destructures this type is in the blast radius. | Risk: HIGH | Why: structural type change; two new fields propagate through 3 downstream modules (ordinal_mapper.py, disclosure_types.py, disclosure_handler.py) and 2 test files that construct it directly.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py::_build_entries` (line 128) - Root-cause site: `return [NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=entry[\\\"content\\\"]) for i, entry in enumerate(section[\\\"entries\\\"])]`. `entry` here is a `SectionEntryDict` (TypedDict with `id`, `struck`, `content` \u2014 see `models.py:1371`) so the struck/id data is already present in the input and is simply not read. The fix reads two more keys from an object that already carries them \u2014 no upstream schema change needed. | Risk: HIGH | Why: this is the exact line the item's reproduction pins as the defect; every caller of `ItemContentNormalizer.normalize()` inherits whatever shape this function emits.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::_build_sections_metadata` (line 2207) and `_build_sections_from_yaml_item` (line 2144) - Both already build `SectionEntryDict(id=e.id, struck=e.struck, content=e.content)` correctly \u2014 struck state is parsed by `entry_blocks.py` and carried faithfully into `ViewItemResult.sections` before `content_normalizer.py` ever sees it. Confirmed by direct read: no bug here. | Risk: LOW | Why: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE only \u2014 these functions are the correct upstream source of the `struck`/`id` data the fix needs; they require no code change, only confirmation they remain untouched.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py::OrdinalPathMapper` (`build_map()`, `resolve()`, `_index_entry_subtree()`, `_SubtreeNode`, `ResolvedUnit`, `OrdinalEntry`) - This is the largest undocumented piece of the blast radius \u2014 not named in the item body, found by tracing `NormalizedSection.entries` consumption. Three types here (`OrdinalEntry`, `ResolvedUnit`, the internal `_SubtreeNode`) carry `content`/`title`/`total_tokens` but have no `struck`/`id` fields, so struck-state is lost a second time even after `NormalizedEntry` is fixed, unless these are also extended. Worse: `build_map()` line 358 does `section_content = \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\".join(e.content for e in section.entries)` \u2014 at level-1 (section) granularity, struck and live entry content is **already concatenated into one string with no separator or marker** before any token counting or ordinal assignment happens. Fixing `NormalizedEntry` alone does not fix what a level-1 `navigate`/`extract` call returns for a mixed section \u2014 that requires a design decision here (skip struck content from the joined string? mark it inline? emit only for `show=all`?), not just field-threading. | Risk: HIGH | Why: (compatibility) 3 internal types need new fields to avoid re-dropping struck state one layer downstream; (behavioral) level-1 section-content aggregation has no design for mixed struck/live content today and silently merges them \u2014 this is a second instance of the same defect class the item reports, one layer removed.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py::BacklogViewDisclosureHandler` (`_handle_navigate`, `_handle_extract`, `_handle_map`) - Constructs `MapResponse`/`NavigateResponse`/`BoundedResponse` directly from `OrdinalEntry`/`ResolvedUnit` fields (e.g. `content=unit.content`, `title=unit.title`). Once `ResolvedUnit`/`OrdinalEntry` carry `struck`/`id`, this handler must be updated to pass them through into the response dataclasses \u2014 it is pure plumbing today with no struck-awareness. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: mechanical propagation once upstream types change; low design risk but a required code change at every response-construction call site (3 handler methods).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_execute_disclosure_or_passthrough` (line 2360) and `backlog_view` tool (line 2392) - Returns `dataclasses.asdict(response)` verbatim as the MCP tool result \u2014 this is the actual wire payload the calling agent receives. No transformation happens here, so once the response dataclasses gain `struck`/`id` fields they appear automatically; no code change needed at this call site itself. The `backlog_view` docstring (lines 2553-2559) enumerates the current response shape per mode (`map=True`, `navigate=`, `navigate=+head=`) without `struck`/`id` \u2014 this must be updated so the tool's own instruction text tells calling agents to check the new field. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (operational) the JSON pass-through is safe/additive, but (documentation) the tool description is the primary way an agent discovers response shape and currently promises a shape that will be incomplete once the fix ships.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\nNone identified \u2014 no third module independently re-drops `struck`/`id` in a way not already covered above. Checked and ruled out: `operations.py`'s other section builders (`_build_sections_metadata`, `_build_sections_from_yaml_item`) both carry `struck` correctly (see Producers). `rendering.py` and `github_sync.py`'s `merge_entries`/`_merge_entries` (grep hits for \\\"struck\\\") operate on `Entry` objects at the grooming/sync layer, a separate pipeline from progressive disclosure \u2014 not implicated in this defect.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py::_make_sections` (line 118) - Constructs `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` directly with only 2 positional/keyword args. This helper backs ~50+ test methods across the file (`groomed_body_doc`, `single_entry_doc`, `empty_section_doc`, `structured_entry_doc`, etc.). If `struck`/`id` are added as required fields with no defaults, every one of these tests fails at construction time before any assertion runs. | Risk: HIGH | Why: (verification) this is not \\\"a test needs a new assertion\\\" \u2014 it is \\\"this fixture helper cannot construct its central input type,\\\" which blocks the entire file's ~50 tests from collecting/running until updated, regardless of whether the tests are about struck entries.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` - No `struck` references anywhere in this file (confirmed by grep). Zero existing coverage asserts what a caller receives (via `MapResponse`/`NavigateResponse`/`BoundedResponse`) when a section contains a struck entry, at any ordinal depth (level-1 section aggregate, level-2 entry, or deeper sub-heading). | Risk: HIGH | Why: (verification) this is the exact gap the item's own reproduction demonstrates \u2014 no reproduction-shaped test exists today (a section with one struck and one live entry, read via `navigate=`/`map=`), so nothing currently proves the fix actually closes the defect end-to-end through the full disclosure path rather than only at the `content_normalizer.py` unit level.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py::TestNormalizedEntryStructure` (line 249) and helper `_entry_dict`/`_section` (lines 63-71) - The test fixtures already build `SectionEntryDict(id=entry_id, struck=False, content=content)`, so struck/id data is already flowing into these tests' inputs \u2014 but `TestNormalizedEntryStructure.test_entries_have_correct_index_and_content` only asserts `isinstance` + `index`, never `struck`/`id`/`content` equality on the output `NormalizedEntry`. No existing test exercises a struck (`struck=True`) input entry at all in this file. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (verification) fixtures are struck-capable already but no test varies `struck` or asserts on it \u2014 new assertions are needed, not new fixtures.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` - Named in `AGENTS.md` as \\\"the mechanical reference for ordinal addressing, navigation parameters, and response shapes\\\" for this exact pipeline. Contains zero mentions of `struck` (confirmed by grep) \u2014 it documents `MapResponse`/`NavigateResponse`/`BoundedResponse` shapes without the new fields. Not currently wrong (the fields don't exist yet), but becomes incomplete the moment the fix ships unless updated in the same change. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (documentation) this is the canonical reference doc for exactly the response shapes being modified; leaving it unupdated means the authoritative contract doc silently drifts from the code the day the fix merges.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` (lines 102-106) - Explicitly enumerates exact response field lists for `backlog_view`: `include_content=False` returns `{title, priority, issue, plan, file_path, groomed, sections_metadata, messages, warnings}`; `map=True` returns `{selector, total_sections, total_est_tokens, map_text, over_budget}`. This is agent-facing instruction content (an AI reads this SKILL.md to know what fields to expect), and it is an exact, closed field list \u2014 not \\\"at least these fields.\\\" Once `struck`/`id`-bearing fields are added to any of these response shapes, this enumeration becomes incomplete. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (documentation, agent-instruction) exact field-list claims in agent-facing instruction content are load-bearing \u2014 an agent following this doc literally would not know to look for a new field even though the JSON itself is additive and safe to ignore.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified \u2014 no config, workflow, or CI file references `NormalizedEntry`, `content_normalizer.py`, `ordinal_mapper.py`, or `disclosure_types.py` by name (confirmed by grep across the plugin).\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_view` docstring (lines ~2519-2559) - Covered under Code - Consumers above (server.py) since the docstring lives in the same function; restated here because it is the primary agent-facing instruction surface for this exact tool (every calling agent reads this description to learn `map=`/`navigate=`/`head=` semantics and response shape). | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: (agent-instruction) the docstring must gain an explicit statement of what `struck`/`id` means and how a struck entry is now represented (omitted / flagged / included-only-on-request \u2014 per the item's own \\\"User-provided context,\\\" this is a grooming-time design decision that the docstring must then describe accurately once made).\\n\\nNone identified beyond the above \u2014 `skills/backlog/README.md` and `skills/complete-implementation/references/final-handoff.md` also matched a \\\"struck\\\" grep but concern a different mechanism (grooming-layer `strike_entry`/`merge_entries` audit trail, and post-implementation markdown-strikethrough concern-tracking, respectively) \u2014 neither describes the progressive-disclosure read path and neither is contradicted by this change.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/content_normalizer.py` | Role: producer | Connection: defines `NormalizedEntry`/`NormalizedSection` and the root-cause `_build_entries` function that drops `struck`/`id` | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'NormalizedEntry'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: consumes `NormalizedSection.entries`; builds `OrdinalEntry`/`ResolvedUnit`/`_SubtreeNode` with no struck/id fields; concatenates entry content at section level with no struck/live separation | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_types.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: `MapResponse`, `NavigateResponse`, `BoundedResponse`, `BoundedContent` dataclasses are the MCP response shapes that must gain struck/id-derived fields | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/disclosure_handler.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: `BacklogViewDisclosureHandler` constructs disclosure response dataclasses from `ResolvedUnit`/`OrdinalEntry` fields at 3 call sites | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | Role: producer (upstream, verified correct) | Connection: `_build_sections_metadata` and `_build_sections_from_yaml_item` already carry `struck`/`id` correctly into `SectionEntryDict` | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | Role: consumer + agent-instruction | Connection: `backlog_view` tool passes disclosure responses through via `dataclasses.asdict`; tool docstring documents response shape without struck/id | Action: MULTIPLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py` | Role: test | Connection: `_make_sections` helper constructs `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` directly; backs ~50+ test methods that will fail to construct fixtures if new fields are required without defaults | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'NormalizedEntry\\\\('\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` | Role: test | Connection: zero existing struck-entry coverage anywhere in the file; no test proves the item's reproduction scenario is fixed end-to-end through MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_content_normalizer.py` | Role: test | Connection: fixtures already carry struck/id via `SectionEntryDict` but no assertion exercises struck=True or checks the new `NormalizedEntry` fields | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: canonical mechanical reference for the exact response shapes being modified; currently silent on struck/id | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: enumerates exact, closed response field lists for `backlog_view` modes that will gain fields | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ordinal_mapper.py` - carries the same defect class one layer downstream of the reported bug (section-level content concatenation has no struck/live separation), and 3 internal types need new fields; not mentioned in the original item body \u2014 found only by tracing the actual consumer chain.\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_ordinal_mapper.py` - a single fixture helper (`_make_sections`) backs the majority of this file's tests via direct `NormalizedEntry(index=i, content=c)` construction; adding required fields without defaults blocks the whole file, not just struck-related tests.\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/unit/test_disclosure_handler.py` - zero existing coverage of struck-entry behavior anywhere in the MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT response path; the item's own reproduction is not currently expressible as a passing/failing test in this file.\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - compatibility (structural dataclass change propagates through 2 downstream modules before reaching MCP callers)\\n  - behavioral (section-level content aggregation in `ordinal_mapper.py` has no design today for mixed struck/live content \u2014 a second instance of the reported defect class)\\n  - verification (two test files have structural or coverage gaps that block or fail to prove the fix)\\n  - documentation (one canonical contract doc and one agent-instruction SKILL.md both enumerate response shapes that will go stale)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The item body names `content_normalizer.py` and \\\"the response types downstream\\\" generically. Tracing the actual consumer chain surfaces `ordinal_mapper.py` (`OrdinalEntry`, `ResolvedUnit`, `_SubtreeNode`) and `disclosure_handler.py` as concrete, previously-unnamed members of the blast radius, plus a second latent instance of the same defect class in `ordinal_mapper.py`'s section-level content-joining. Planning and fact-check scope should be expanded to explicitly include `ordinal_mapper.py`'s section-content-aggregation behavior, not just the `NormalizedEntry` field addition.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation during messy-read risk analysis, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproduction\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun against the parsing and 